-- title:
--   Invertible ideal sheaves give invertible modules
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and let $I$ be an ideal sheaf datum on $X$ (a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals of $\mathcal O_X$ recorded by its ideals $I.\mathrm{ideal}(U) \subseteq \Gamma(X,U)$ on affine opens). Assume `I.IsInvertible`: for every point $x$ of $X$ there are an affine open $U$ of $X$ and a section $f \in \Gamma(X,U)$ with $x \in X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\, f$, together with an element $g$ of $\Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f)$ which is a non-zero-divisor and generates $I$ there, i.e. $I.\mathrm{ideal}(X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f) = (g)$. The conclusion is that the $\mathcal O_X$-module $I.\mathrm{module}$ — defined as the kernel of the canonical map from the unit sheaf of modules $\mathcal O_X$ to the pushforward along the closed immersion $I.\mathrm{subscheme}\iota$ of the unit sheaf of modules of the closed subscheme cut out by $I$ — satisfies `Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible`: every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ such that the pullback of $I.\mathrm{module}$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic, as a sheaf of modules on $U$, to the unit sheaf of modules $\mathcal O_U$. Only the assertion for $I.\mathrm{module}$ is made; nothing is claimed here about its dual.
--
--   This is the statement that for a locally principal ideal sheaf generated by non-zero-divisors, the ideal sheaf $\mathcal I = \mathcal O_X(-Z)$ is a line bundle, multiplication by the local generator trivialising it. It is the basic input for treating relative effective Cartier divisors on curves as line bundles, and is used throughout the divisor-theoretic part of the curve and relative Picard functor development.
